  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nHeat persists through early next week:\n\nThe stationary front will finally be removed from our area\nstarting Saturday. Furthermore, the mid-level trough axis will\nshift east of our region, producing more sinking air and rising\nheights/pressure. This will decrease any significant chance of\nrain and storms and bring persistent heat to the region through\nat least Tuesday/Wednesday next week. NBM 25-75th temperatures\nspreads continue to forecast highs in the 90s during this\nperiod, with some locations across our south and west\napproaching 100 F. Current dewpoint forecasts aren't expected to\nbe overly \"excessive\" with values in the upper 60s to lower 70s.\nTherefore, Heat Index values won't be \"extreme\" (>110 F), and\ncurrent NBM/LREF ensembles put <15% chance for >105 F. However,\nthere are moderately-high (60-70%) chances for Heat Index values\nbetween 100-105 to occur from Saturday through at least\nTuesday/Wednesday. Persistent heat like this along with little\nrelief with lows in the lower to middle 70s will elevate heat\nrisk as we go through next week. This may require a Heat\nAdvisory by Monday or Tuesday. \n\nDuring this period, weather is expected to be dry for most\nplaces. However, we won't exactly be under a ridge/heat dome.\nAnd with moisture being plentiful, isolated showers and\nthunderstorms would still be possible each day. Some medium\nrange guidance suggests this to be the case east of Highway 65\nSaturday afternoon despite chances of precipitation being <15%.\n\n\nPattern change to bring several chances of rain late next week:\n\nAs we get into the middle to latter periods of next week, global\nensembles bring the jet stream a bit more south, enhancing\nnorthwesterly flow over our region. Any shortwaves that move\nthrough the flow could bring multiple chances of rain. Current\nforecasts place 30-60% chances across the region Wednesday and\nonwards. The CPC gives us a 40-50% chance for seeing above\nnormal precipitation between August 12-16. Since normal\nprecipitation for this time period is around 0.5-0.75 inches,\nthere is a 40-50% chance for exceeding that amount between\nAugust 12-16.",
